# [[.Title]]

A Steward provider for [[.Title]], built with the [Go SDK](https://github.com/treno-dev/steward-sdk/tree/main/go).

## Run

```sh
go mod tidy
steward provider validate go run .
```

A provider is started by a runner, not by hand: run directly it says so and exits. Use
`steward provider validate` to check it, and `steward provider run --context context.json go run .` to invoke
it. Write logs to standard error.

## Test

Save this as `context.json`, the credential and services to call the provider with:

```json
{
  "credential": {
    "inputs": { "base_url": "https://example.test" },
    "secrets": { "API_TOKEN": "secret" }
  },
  "identity": { "external_id": "someone@example.test" },
  "role": { "name": "viewer", "permissions": [{ "name": "view" }] },
  "services": [{ "kind": "item", "name": "demo", "inputs": { "description": "A demo item" } }]
}
```

```sh
steward provider run --context context.json go run .
steward provider run --context context.json --call provision go run .
```

The first creates each service, grants and revokes the role, and removes it. It does this for real, in
whatever your secrets reach, and asks before it starts (`--yes` skips the question). `--call` makes
only the calls you name, once each and without cleaning up: `provision`, `grant-access`, `get-access`,
`revoke-access` and `deprovision`. `steward provider validate` never changes anything.

## Where things are

`main.go` declares the provider's inputs (what it needs to connect), each kind of service and the handlers
Steward calls: `Provision`, `Deprovision`, `GrantAccess`, `RevokeAccess` and `GetAccess`. Handlers take
and return the typed messages of the contract. Leave out the handlers you do not need: Steward offers
only what you implement.

To add a kind of service, call `p.Service("kind", provider.ServiceOptions{...})` again with its own
inputs, outputs and handlers.

See the [SDK README](https://github.com/treno-dev/steward-sdk/blob/main/go/README.md) for
everything a provider can declare.
